          "settings": {
            "eyebrow": "",
            "heading": "What is espresso?",
            "body": "<p>Espresso isn't a bean or a roast — it's a method. A pump drives hot water through finely-ground, tightly-packed coffee at high pressure (around nine bars), extracting a concentrated ounce or two topped with a layer of golden <em>crema</em>. That intensity is what makes espresso the building block for milk drinks and long blacks alike.</p>",
            "narrow": false
          }
        },
        "b2_image_text": {
          "type": "image_text",
          "settings": {
            "image": "shopify://shop_images/pressure-pull-espresso.jpg",
            "image_position": "left",
            "eyebrow": "How it works",
            "heading": "Pressure is everything",
            "body": "<p>Where drip relies on gravity, espresso relies on force. The machine heats water to the ideal temperature and pushes it through the grounds in about 25–30 seconds. The result is a small, syrupy shot with far more flavor and body than regular coffee.</p>",
            "button_label": "Shop espresso machines",
            "button_link": "shopify://collections/coffee-espresso-makers"
          }
        },
        "b3_image_text": {
          "type": "image_text",
          "settings": {
            "image": "shopify://shop_images/espresso-drinks.jpg",
            "image_position": "right",
            "eyebrow": "The drinks",
            "heading": "One shot, endless drinks",
            "body": "<p>Master the shot and you've unlocked an entire menu:</p><ul><li><strong>Latte</strong> — espresso plus lots of steamed milk</li><li><strong>Cappuccino</strong> — equal parts espresso, steamed milk, and foam</li><li><strong>Flat white</strong> — espresso plus velvety microfoam</li><li><strong>Americano</strong> — espresso plus hot water</li></ul>",
            "button_label": "",
            "button_link": ""
          }
        },
        "b4_rich_text": {
          "type": "rich_text",
          "settings": {
            "eyebrow": "",
            "heading": "Manual, semi-automatic, or automatic?",
            "body": "<p>Espresso machines range from hands-on to hands-off:</p><ul><li><strong>Semi-automatic</strong> — you control the shot, the machine controls pressure. The sweet spot for most home baristas.</li><li><strong>Automatic</strong> — one-touch shots and milk for café drinks with less practice.</li><li><strong>Built-in grinder</strong> — grind fresh beans right into the portafilter for the best possible flavor.</li></ul>",
            "narrow": false
          }
        },
        "b5_callout": {
          "type": "callout",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Pro tip",
            "body": "<p>Fresh, evenly-ground beans and a good tamp matter more than any single setting. If your shot pulls too fast and tastes sour, grind finer; too slow and bitter, grind coarser. Small tweaks, big difference.</p>"
          }
        },
        "b6_image_text": {
          "type": "image_text",
          "settings": {
            "image": "shopify://shop_images/cappuccino-chefman-espresso.jpg",
            "image_position": "left",
            "eyebrow": "Milk drinks",
            "heading": "Steam, froth, repeat",
            "body": "<p>A built-in steam wand or automatic frother turns cold milk into silky microfoam for lattes and cappuccinos. It's the difference between a plain shot and a proper coffee-shop drink at home.</p>",
            "button_label": "",
            "button_link": ""
          }
        },
        "b7_rich_text": {
          "type": "rich_text",
          "settings": {
            "eyebrow": "",
            "heading": "What to look for",
            "body": "<ul><li><strong>Pressure</strong> — a pump that delivers consistent pressure for a proper extraction.</li><li><strong>Milk system</strong> — a steam wand for control, or an automatic frother for convenience.</li><li><strong>Grinder</strong> — built-in saves counter space and keeps grounds fresh.</li><li><strong>Footprint</strong> — espresso machines run larger, so measure your counter first.</li></ul>",
            "narrow": false
          }
        },
        "b8_callout": {
          "type": "callout",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Good to know",
            "body": "<p>Espresso has the steepest learning curve of the three — but also the highest ceiling. Give yourself a week of practice and you'll be pulling shots you'd happily pay five dollars for.</p>"
          }
        }
      },
